Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed via the selfassembly of metal ions (or clusters) and organic ligands, have attracted extensive attention because of their diverse topologies, tunable functionalities, and large surface areas.[1-4] These characteristics  make  MOFs  highly potential in various applications such as catalysis, adsorption, and sensing.[5-11l It is noticeable that most reported MOFs are microporous with pore sizes less than $2\mathsf{n m}$ ， which favors the capture/separation of guests with small molecular sizes. However, the access of larger molecules to interior pores is hindered, which significantly limits their applications in some cases.[12-14] Hence, the fabrication of MOFs with larger pore diameters is highly expected, yet still challenging until now.
Two predominant methods, namely direct synthesis and post treatment, have been developed to dilate the pore sizes of MOFs.[15-27] For direct synthesis, larger pores are formed during the crystallization of frameworks. A typical method is the use of elongated organic ligands.[28,29] Despite the success in several MOF systems, interpenetration of structures is easy to take place, which reduces the pore sizes sharply. Moreover, the elongation of organic ligands compromises the stability of MOFs. The templating approach is a fascinating alternative of direct synthesis to fabricate hierarchically porous metal-organic frameworks (HPMOFs). The list of the templates has involved surfactants[30-34] nanoparticles[35]  and  metal-organic  assemblies,[36,37]  while suitable interaction between the templates and MOFs need to be designed meticulously. In addition, perturbation-assisted preparation is utilized, in which MOFs are formed with dynamically restricted nucleation of crystals, leading to the formation of hierarchical pores at the expense of MOF crystallinity[38,39] For the fabrication of HP-MOFs by post treatment, the main idea is the partial degradation of microporous MOFs by utilizing their instability under some specific conditions.[40,41] For example, microporous MOFs can be first constructed through the coordination  of  Zr  nodes  and pro-labile  ligand 4- carboxybenzylidene-4-aminobenzate, and mesopores are then generated as crystal defects via the cleavage of pro-labile ligands.[42] Although such methods are attractive, the design of MOFs with special vulnerable moieties is required, which is difficult to be generalized. Therefore, despite great efforts, the development of a facile method to fabricate HP-MOFs remains a pronounced challenge.
It is known that most of the transition metallic ions can exist in multiple valence states (e.g. $1+$ and $^{2+}$ for Cu), which correspond to different coordination modes with organic ligands. Once a MOF is synthesized, the valence state is definite for metallic ions.The modulation of cation valence may lead to the change of coordination mode and the subsequent dissociation between metal ions and ligands. If the amount of dissociated bonds/ligands can be controlled, hierarchical pores are supposed to form in the originally microporous MOFs. To our knowledge, this strategy has never been used for the preparation of HP-MOFs up to now.
Herein, we report a strategy, namely the modulation of cation valence, to create hierarchical porosity in MOFs. As a proof of concept, HKUST-1 with $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ metal nodes was selected as the original microporous MOF. The modulation of Cu valence was realized by reducing vapor treatment (RVT). With temperature increasing,the generated reducing vapor of $C H_{3}O H$ diffuses into the framework of HKUST-1 and interacts with $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ ions, leading to the transformation of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ (Figure 1A). This changes the original coordination mode of Cu nodes and partially breaks coordination bonds, resulting in the formation of mesopores and eventual macropores in the original microporous HKUST-1. Moreover, the RVT is more controllable in comparison with liquid processes,which makes it easy to obtain MOFs with tunable amount of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ and subsequent hierarchical pores formation. The synthesized HP-MOFs are denoted as HK-n (n, the RVT duration varied from 0 to $12\mathsf{h}$ ，and original HKUST-1 was also represented as HK-0 for convenience. The HK-n containing $\mathsf{C u}(|)$ sites are found to be efficient in thiophene capture, and the saturated adsorption capacity is up to $0.406\mathsf{m m o l}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ ,which is six times higher than that of the HK-0 (0.069 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ ）
The $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the HK-n samples agree well with each other (Figure 2A),[43] suggesting that the crystalline main structure of HK-0 is not qualitatively damaged upon RVT. After RVT, the characteristic peaks of O-coordinated $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ are observed,[44] indicating that Cu(ll) are successfully reduced to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ . The valence states of Cu in HK-n were checked with X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS, Figure 2B). The characteristic peak at $934.8\mathsf{e V}$ of HK-0 ascribed to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ is found along with a tiny shoulder peak at $932.6~\mathsf{e V}$ that is attributed to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ ，suggesting $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ species are predominant in HK-0.[45] With RVT duration increased, the peak at $932.6~{\tt e V}$ becomes intense progressively, revealing that the portion of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ is enhanced.For example, the content of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ in HK-10 based on XPS deconvolution analysis reaches $44\%$ . Further quantification of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ based on titration is given in Table S1, which is in agreement with the XPS results. The reduction of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ was also confirmed by UV-vis spectra (Figure S1). The results demonstrate that the RVT strategy can modulate the valence state of Cu in HKUST-1，and the content of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ can be well tailored by adjusting the RVT duration. A
The porosity of the materials was studied by ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption at 77 K. HK-0 gives an isotherm of type-l, and the rapid ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake at low $p/p_{0}$ interval indicates the microporosity (Figure 3A). Upon RVT the shape of isotherms changes obviously. For HK-8 and $H K-10$ ， type-lV isotherms with H2-type hysteresis loops are observed， which are the characteristics of ink-bottle-like mesopores with narrow entrance and large cavity. The ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake of HK-12, however, declines sharply, which is due to the framework collapse caused by excessive RVT duration. The pore size distributions shown in Figure 3B reveal that the size of mesopores is centered at about 3, 20, and $30\mathsf{n m}$ respectively for HK-6,HK-8, and HK-10. These results exhibit that hierarchical meso- and micropores are successfully formed through RVT,and the hierarchical pore sizes are tunable with different RVT durations. The abundant meso- and macropores in the crystal can be observed directly through scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figures 4A and S2). The elemental mappings indicate that all related elementals are homogenously dispersed in the sample (Figure 4B). In addition, the optical microscope images show that the color of samples changes gradually from blue to dark green (Figure S3), which supports the fact of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$
formation. Thermogravimetric (TG) analysis shows that the thermal stability of HK-0 is weakened by RVT because of the formation of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ species and the variation of coordination mode (Figure S4).
Due to increasingly stricter environmental regulations worldwide, deep desulfurization of hydrocarbon fuels becomes urgent.[46] Traditional hydrodesulfurization technique is efficent in the removal of aliphatic sulfides but less effective for aromatic ones. Thus, it is promising that adsorptive desulfurization selectively removes aromatic sulfides at ambient temperature. $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ -containing adsorbents are considered to be one of the most attractive candidates for adsorptive desulfurization owing to the robust T-complexation interaction between the $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ sites and aromatic sulfides[47] Besides, hierarchical pore structures are regarded as dynamically favorable for adsorptive desulfurization in view of the average molecular size of aromatic sulfides. The HK-n are thus considered to be employed for the adsorption of a typical aromatic sulfide, thiophene, in view of their tunable amount of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ and the developed hierarchical pore structures upon RVT. A fixed-bed adsorption system was utilized to evaluate the desulfurization preformance, and breakthrough curves of thiophene were recorded at ambient temperature (Figures 5 and S5). Becuase of the weak interaction between $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ and thiophene, HK-O gives a poor adsorption capacity on thiophene (0.069 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}.$ . In contrast, the adsorption capacity of $H K-10$ reaches $0.406{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}$ 。which is around six times higher than that of HK-O. It is noticeable that $H K-12$ with the highest content of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ can only capture 0.171 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ of thiophene becuase of excessive structural degradation. In addition, regenerability of the adsorbents was carried out and shown in Figure S6. After four cycles, HK-10 still exhibits a good adsorption capacity comparable to the fresh adsorbent, suggesting no loss in adsorption activity. These results indicate that both the valence states of Cu and pore structures affect the performances of adsorptive desulfurization, and HK-10 possessing both suitable amount of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ and developed hierarchical pore structures performs best among the materials.
To examine the formation process of hierarchical porosity of HK-n, Fourier transformed infrared (FTlR) and Raman spectra of the samples were collected. The FTlR results indicate the cleavage of coordination bonds and the formation of dissociative ligands (Figure S7); the Raman spectra reveal the decrease of Cu(ll)-O vibration and the appearence of Cu(l)-O vibration (Figure S8). The underlying mechanism of RVT is revealed with the firstprinciple calculation. The computational details are given in the supporting information (Sl). A reactant cluster of dual-Cu(ll) coordinated with quadruple $-\mathsf{C O O^{\star}}$ groups is employed to simulate the representative node of HKUsT-1 in the course of RVT. The reaction pathway involving all the intermediates (INT) and transition states (TS) is shown in Figure 6. Once the reducing agent, i.e., $C H_{3}O H$ , is chemisorbed onto the Cu(ll) site along the Z-axis, it is facile for the coordinated carboxyl groups to be squeezed out with a tiny energy barrier $(4.0\ \mathsf{k c a l/m o l})$ ，owing to the replacement by the $C H_{3}O H$ (the stage from Reactant to INT1). In fact, this step has shown a potential course of HKUST-1 dissociation in $C H_{3}O H$ It is fortunate that RVT effectively avoids the large-scale dissociation through vapor-phase reaction, and besides, according to the calculation, the $C H_{3}O H$ replacement will be closely followed by the reduction reaction. As the stage from INT1 to INT2 exhibits, with the H atom of alcoholic hydroxyl group transferred onto a $-\mathsf{C O O^{\star}}$ group, the $C H_{3}O H$ is oxidized to be $C H_{3}O^{\star}$ ， and meanwhile the original dual-Cu(ll) node is half reduced through accepting one electron. As a result, it is possible for INT2 to decrease its energy through electron-spin modulation to generate INT3 and INT4, successively. Note that the -COOH group that was generated through hydrogen transference gradually moves away from the newly formed dual-Cu(lS) node at this stage, because of the coordination disability. With the methylic H atom of $C H_{3}O^{\star}$ delivered to another $-\mathsf{C O O^{\star}}$ group to form the second -COOH, the $C H_{3}O^{\star}$ is finally oxidized to be HCHO (from INT4 to Product). Meanwhile, dual-Cu(IS) node is ultimately reduced to be dual-Cu(l) through accepting the second electron. The rate-determining step with the highest energy barrier of $20.2\ \mathsf{k c a l/m o l}$ on the main pathway has to be jumped here. Such moderate energy barrier is the real reason that enables the RVT reaction, meanwhile moderates the reaction rate Therefore, the RVT effects can be clearly distinguished through tunable RVT duration by the hour. Another minor pathway is found as well (from INT2 to INT4'), in which the electron-spin modulation took place after the second hydrogen transference.However, the energy barrier is higher than that of the main pathway, so the latter is optimal. As for the product, in contrast to the quadruple coodination mode of the original dua/-Cu(ll) node, the reduced dual-Cu(l) node is coordinated and stabllized with only two $-\mathsf{C O O^{\star}}$ groups, surrounded by the newly formed by-products micomolecules, i.e.， -COOH species and HCHO. With the release of the micromolecules, hierarchical pores are finally constrcuted. To further examine the generality of the RVT mechanism for the construction of HP-MOFs, the RVT with another weak reducing agent, ethanol,was tested, of which details are given in the SI (Figure S9 to S14).
In conclusion, we have developed a facile and efficient strategy to create hierarchical porosity in a typical MOF, HKUST1, by modulating cation valence. The Cu(ll) nodes in the MOF can be transformed to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ via the RVT， which changes the coordination mode and breaks coordination bonds partially, leading to the formation of hierarchical pores in microporous frameworks. Due to the good controllability of RVT, it is easy to tailor the amount of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ and subsequent hierarchical porosity. The resultant HP-HKUST-1 presents obviously enhanced activity in the capture of aromatic sulfide in contrast to the original microporous HKUST-1. The present valence modulation strategy may open up an avenue for the generation of hierarchical porosity in MOFs for diverse applications in adsorption and catalysis, etc. Work is in progress to explore other reducing agents like isopropanol and even mixed solvent systems.
